## Why CC BY rather than MIT

The written half of this repository is not documentation for the code — it is
the analysis. Findings documents, the methodology note, the decisions index.
Applying a software licence to those is a category error, and the terms that
matter for analysis are the ones CC BY states: credit, and an indication of
changes.

There is a concrete reason this clause is here rather than assumed. An
unsourced editorial claim in this repo was forked, indexed, and eventually
returned by a search engine as corroboration *for itself* — the claim had
become its own evidence, with the provenance stripped somewhere along the way
(recorded in `session-summary/2026-09-20-s176.md`). A licence cannot prevent
that. What it can do is make "say where this came from, and say what you
changed" the stated term of reuse rather than a courtesy that erodes at each
hop.

